          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Best Air Mattress for Guests: Height, Capacity and a Firm Edge
          </h2>

          <p>
            A guest bed has one job: give a visitor a decent night in a room that is not really a bedroom. Four specs separate a good guest air mattress from a bad one, and all of them appear on the listings, so you can compare before you buy.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Height</h3>

          <p>
            The taller the bed, the more it feels like a real bed and the easier it is to get in and out of. Our raised picks run from 18 inches for the Intex twin to 22 inches for the Intex Comfort Plush queen. For parents, grandparents or anyone who finds rising from a low bed awkward, look for 18 inches or more.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Weight Capacity</h3>

          <p>
            Capacity tells you how much load the maker rates the bed for. Queens in this guide are listed between 600 and 661 pounds, and the King Koil queen at 650 pounds is the highest of the raised guest beds. Twins are rated lower, around 300 to 350 pounds, which means one sleeper. Stay well inside the rating; the edges take more stress than the middle when someone sits down.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Internal Support and Edges</h3>

          <p>
            Air mattresses use internal coils or beams to stop the surface ballooning into a round tube. SoundAsleep lists 40 air coils, King Koil pairs coil beams with a reinforced edge frame, and Intex uses its Dura-Beam system. A firmer edge matters because guests sit on the side of the bed to put on shoes or get up.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Top Surface and Grip</h3>

          <p>
            A flocked top holds sheets in place and feels less like plastic. On hard floors, the bottom matters too: the SoundAsleep's SureGrip bottom is designed to stop the bed creeping across hardwood or tile overnight.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="camping" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. The Best Air Mattress for Camping: Power, Height and Packed Size
          </h2>

          <p>
            Camping changes the priorities. A 22-inch guest bed is too tall for most tents and its pump needs an outlet, so the best air mattress for camping is usually lower, simpler and able to inflate without mains power.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Campsites Without Power</h3>

          <p>
            If there is no electric hookup, choose a bed you can inflate on site. The Bestway Tritech's built-in D-cell battery pump is the easiest option, and the Intex Classic Downy's included hand pump is the most dependable, since it has no motor or batteries to fail. Pack spare batteries and the repair patch either way.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Campsites With Hookups and Cabins</h3>

          <p>
            At a site with electricity, a double-high bed like the Coleman SupportRest is comfortable and quick to set up. Without hookups, a 120V pump can run from a portable power station; our guide to the <a href="/best-portable-power-station-for-camping/">best portable power stations for camping</a> covers units with AC outlets. A USB power bank, like those in our <a href="/best-camping-power-bank/">camping power bank guide</a>, will not run a 120V pump.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check the Tent Floor and Headroom</h3>

          <p>
            A queen airbed is about 80 by 60 inches, and many tents sold as four-person barely fit one with room to move. Check the tent's floor dimensions, and remember that a taller bed means less headroom when you sit up.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Insulate Underneath</h3>

          <p>
            Air inside the mattress takes on the temperature of the ground, so an airbed can feel cold on chilly nights. A blanket or foam pad between you and the mattress, and another underneath it, makes a noticeable difference.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Queen vs Twin Air Mattress: What Size Do You Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Air mattresses follow standard mattress sizes, so the choice is the same one you would make for a real bed, with two extra questions: how big is the room, and how many people will share it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Queen: The Default for Guests</h3>

          <p>
            A queen air mattress is about 80 by 60 inches. It fits two adults, works with standard queen fitted sheets and is the size most people mean when they search for the best queen air mattress. Five of our seven picks are queens. Before you order, measure the room with the furniture in it; King Koil itself tells buyers to check that the space fits 80 by 60 inches.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Twin: One Sleeper, Small Spaces</h3>

          <p>
            A twin suits one guest, a child or a narrow office. The King Koil twin is listed at 76 by 42 inches, and twin capacities run around 300 to 350 pounds. The best twin air mattress with a built-in pump still gives you a raised height, as the 18-inch Intex Deluxe twin does.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Full and King</h3>

          <p>
            Full airbeds sit between the two, and kings give couples more space but need a large room. King Koil sells full and California king versions of its raised bed, Intex sells a full Comfort Plush and EnerPlex sells a king; the advice in this guide applies to them too.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="holds-air" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Does an Air Mattress Hold Air? Why New Beds Seem to Leak
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most common complaint about any air mattress is that it was flat by morning. Often the bed is not leaking at all. Three things make a sound mattress feel softer overnight.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">New PVC Stretches for the First Few Nights</h3>

          <p>
            Vinyl stretches when it is first inflated, and as it stretches the pressure inside drops. Intex states this plainly on its listings: at first inflation the material stretches, which is sometimes mistaken for a leak, and the bed fully settles within 2 to 3 nights. King Koil similarly advises topping the bed off after the first night as the material relaxes. Re-inflate, and the softening should become smaller each night.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Temperature Drops at Night</h3>

          <p>
            Air contracts as it cools. A bed inflated in a warm afternoon will feel softer in a cooler room by early morning, and the effect is stronger in a tent. Inflate in the room where the bed will be used, and let it reach room temperature before the final top-up.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Real Leak</h3>

          <p>
            If the bed goes noticeably flat night after night, long after the first few nights, or you can hear a hiss, look for a leak at the valve, the seams or a puncture. The next sections cover how to prevent deflation and how to find and patch a hole.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="deflating" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. How to Keep an Air Mattress From Deflating
          </h2>

          <p>
            A few habits make the difference between a bed that holds firm and one that sags by 3 a.m.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Stretch It Before Guests Arrive</h3>

          <p>
            Inflate a new bed fully a day or two before it is needed, let it sit overnight and top it up. Doing the stretching in advance means your guest does not wake on a soft bed on the first night.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Clear the Floor</h3>

          <p>
            Sweep the area and look for staples, splinters, pet nails and gravel. Indoors, put a rug, blanket or the carry bag under the bed; outdoors, use a groundsheet or tarp under the tent.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Do Not Overinflate</h3>

          <p>
            A drum-tight bed puts strain on the seams and is less comfortable. Inflate until it is firm, lie on it, then adjust. Built-in pumps make it easy to fine-tune.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Check the Valve</h3>

          <p>
            Make sure the valve or pump dial is fully closed and the cap is seated. A partly open valve is one of the commonest causes of slow overnight air loss.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 5: Stay Within the Weight Rating</h3>

          <p>
            Do not let children use the bed as a trampoline, and avoid kneeling or standing on one spot. Point loads stress the internal beams far more than lying flat.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="leaks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How to Find and Fix a Leak in an Air Mattress
          </h2>

          <p>
            If the bed keeps losing air after the stretch-in period, find the leak before you buy a replacement. Most holes are small and easy to patch.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Inflate Firmly and Listen</h3>

          <p>
            In a quiet room, inflate the bed fully and press gently on it. Listen for a hiss and run a damp hand slowly over the surface; escaping air feels cool on wet skin. Check the valve and seams first.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Use Soapy Water</h3>

          <p>
            Mix a little dish soap with water and dab it over suspect areas with a sponge, avoiding the pump housing. Bubbles form where air escapes. Mark the spot with a pen once you find it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Patch It Properly</h3>

          <p>
            Deflate the bed, clean and dry the area, then apply the maker's vinyl repair patch, which many airbeds include in the box, following the instructions. Flocked tops can need the flocking lightly roughened or removed first for the patch to stick. Let the adhesive cure for the full recommended time before inflating.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When It Is Not Worth Fixing</h3>

          <p>
            Leaks along a long seam, inside the pump housing or between internal beams are hard to fix at home. If the bed is still under warranty, contact the maker first; King Koil, for example, lists a 1-year warranty.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="setup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. How to Make an Air Mattress Feel Like a Real Guest Bed
          </h2>

          <p>
            A few additions turn an air mattress from a camping fix into a proper guest bed.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Use Real Sheets</h3>

          <p>
            Raised queens are about 80 by 60 inches, so standard queen sheets fit, though taller beds may need deep-pocket fitted sheets. The Intex Classic Downy listing notes that its standard queen size works with fitted sheets.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Add a Topper</h3>

          <p>
            A thin foam or fiber topper adds softness and a layer between the sleeper and the vinyl. Choose one that matches the mattress size; our <a href="/best-mattress-topper/">mattress topper guide</a> compares types, and a flocked top helps keep it in place.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Protect It</h3>

          <p>
            A <a href="/best-mattress-protector/">mattress protector</a> keeps spills off the flocked top and makes the bed easier to clean between visits.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Place It Away From Hazards</h3>

          <p>
            Keep the bed away from radiators, heaters, sharp furniture corners and doors that swing into it, and leave access to the outlet so the pump can top it up.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="everyday" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. Can You Use an Air Mattress Every Day?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Searches for the best air mattress for everyday use are common, usually from people moving house, waiting for furniture or furnishing a first apartment.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">As a Short-Term Bed</h3>

          <p>
            A raised air mattress with a built-in pump works as a temporary bed for weeks. Expect to top it up regularly, keep it on a clean, smooth floor and check the valve often.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Limits</h3>

          <p>
            Air mattresses are designed and warrantied for occasional and temporary use. Daily use speeds up wear on seams and valves, and the bed needs regular adjustment.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When to Buy a Real Mattress</h3>

          <p>
            If you are sleeping on it for more than a short spell, a real mattress is the better buy. Our <a href="/best-mattress/">best mattress guide</a> covers affordable bed-in-a-box options that arrive compressed, much like an air mattress.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="storage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            12. How to Deflate, Fold and Store an Air Mattress
          </h2>

          <p>
            Careful storage keeps a guest bed ready for the next visit.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Deflate Completely</h3>

          <p>
            Use the built-in pump's deflate setting, or open the valve and gently press out the remaining air, folding from the end opposite the valve.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Make Sure It Is Dry</h3>

          <p>
            After camping, wipe off dirt and let the bed dry fully before packing it away, so it does not get musty.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fold Loosely and Store Somewhere Mild</h3>

          <p>
            Fold along the natural lines, avoid sharp creases, and store it in its carry bag in a closet rather than a hot attic or freezing garage.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming a New Bed Is Leaking</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                New PVC stretches over the first few nights, and Intex and King Koil both tell owners to top up the bed. Re-inflate before returning a bed that softened on its first night.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a 120V Pump for a Campsite With No Power</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A built-in AC pump needs an outlet. For tent camping without hookups, choose a battery pump like the Bestway Tritech's or a bed with a hand pump.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Weight Capacity</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Twins are often rated around 300 to 350 pounds, which means one adult. Check the listed capacity, and stay well within it when two people share a queen.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Measuring the Room or Tent</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A queen air mattress takes about 80 by 60 inches of floor. Measure the room with its furniture, or the tent floor, before you order.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Overinflating Until It Is Rock Hard</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A drum-tight bed strains seams and is less comfortable. Inflate until firm, lie on it and adjust with the pump.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

