        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            14. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best air mattress?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homes, the SoundAsleep Dream Series Queen is the best air mattress. Its listing gives a 19-inch raised height, 40 internal air coils, a one-click built-in pump that inflates it in under four minutes and a 600-pound capacity. Choose the King Koil for faster inflation, or the Bestway Tritech for camping without power.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best air mattress for guests?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A raised queen with a built-in pump. The SoundAsleep Dream Series, King Koil and Intex Comfort Plush stand 19 to 22 inches tall, inflate in minutes from an outlet and take standard queen sheets. For one guest in a small room, the Intex Dura-Beam Deluxe twin offers an 18-inch height with a quick built-in pump.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best air mattress for camping?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                One that inflates without mains power and fits your tent. The Bestway Tritech queen has a built-in D-cell battery pump and a 12-inch height, and the Intex Classic Downy includes a hand pump. If your site has electric hookups, a double-high bed like the Coleman SupportRest is also an option.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I keep my air mattress from deflating?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Stretch a new bed in by inflating it a day early and topping it up, inflate it in the room where it will be used, avoid overinflating, check that the valve is fully closed and keep it on a clean floor away from sharp objects. If it still loses air after a few nights, look for a leak.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my new air mattress lose air the first night?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                New PVC stretches when it is first inflated, so the bed feels softer even without a leak. Intex says its beds settle within 2 to 3 nights, and King Koil advises topping off after the first night. Cooler night temperatures also make the air contract. Re-inflate, and the softening should shrink each night.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does it take to inflate an air mattress?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                With a built-in pump, about 2 to 5 minutes for a queen by the makers' figures: about 2 minutes for the King Koil, under 4 for the SoundAsleep and about 4.75 for the 22-inch Intex. Battery pumps take a similar time, like the Bestway's listed 3 minutes, while hand pumps take longer and some effort.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a queen or twin air mattress better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A queen, about 80 by 60 inches, suits two adults and takes standard queen sheets, so it is the better all-round guest bed. A twin suits one sleeper, a child or a small room and is easier to store, but twin capacities run around 300 to 350 pounds. Measure the room before choosing either.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
